1
00:00:00,275 --> 00:00:01,975
Here are the prime numbers.

2
00:00:01,975 --> 00:00:05,500
Two, three, five, seven, eleven, thirteen:

3
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:08,938
the whole numbers that can't be split into smaller factors.

4
00:00:08,938 --> 00:00:14,975
They're the atoms of arithmetic, and yet they seem to appear almost at random.

5
00:00:15,083 --> 00:00:18,908
Count them as you go, and you get a staircase that climbs by one at every

6
00:00:18,908 --> 00:00:19,833
prime.

7
00:00:19,833 --> 00:00:23,150
It's jagged and irregular in its details.

8
00:00:23,150 --> 00:00:27,463
But zoom out, say to a hundred thousand, and the staircase follows a smooth

9
00:00:27,463 --> 00:00:29,613
curve almost perfectly.

10
00:00:29,613 --> 00:00:35,575
The primes are random in the small, but astonishingly regular in the large.

11
00:00:35,708 --> 00:00:39,458
In 1859, Bernhard Riemann found the reason.

12
00:00:39,458 --> 00:00:43,746
He found that the staircase is exactly equal to a smooth curve, plus a sum

13
00:00:43,746 --> 00:00:48,183
of waves, and that each wave comes from a single point where one particular

14
00:00:48,183 --> 00:00:51,967
function, the zeta function, equals zero.

15
00:00:51,967 --> 00:00:56,779
Add the waves one at a time, and the smooth curve bends, step by step, into

16
00:00:56,779 --> 00:00:59,329
the exact staircase of the primes.

17
00:00:59,329 --> 00:01:04,517
The zeros of zeta are like the frequencies in the music of the primes.

18
00:01:05,225 --> 00:01:09,400
Riemann guessed that all of these zeros lie on a single straight line.

19
00:01:09,400 --> 00:01:14,175
That guess, the Riemann hypothesis, is perhaps the most famous open problem

20
00:01:14,175 --> 00:01:15,600
in mathematics.

21
00:01:15,600 --> 00:01:22,450
It has been open for 167 years, and there's a million-dollar prize for a proof.

22
00:01:22,583 --> 00:01:29,171
On October sixth, 2026, OpenAI released 722 mathematical manuscripts written

23
00:01:29,171 --> 00:01:31,671
by an unreleased AI model.

24
00:01:31,671 --> 00:01:35,758
Among them is a claimed proof of something weaker than the Riemann hypothesis,

25
00:01:35,758 --> 00:01:37,308
but long out of reach:

26
00:01:37,308 --> 00:01:39,458
the quasi-Riemann hypothesis.

27
00:01:39,458 --> 00:01:43,458
It says that no zero lies to the right of the line where the real part is

28
00:01:43,458 --> 00:01:45,983
seven eighths.

29
00:01:46,192 --> 00:01:50,292
In this video, we'll build up what the Riemann hypothesis really says, and

30
00:01:50,292 --> 00:01:52,767
why its zeros control the primes.

31
00:01:52,767 --> 00:01:57,292
Then we'll see what the new result claims and how its proof works, and finally

32
00:01:57,292 --> 00:01:59,892
what has been checked, and what it does not prove.

33
00:02:10,564 --> 00:02:13,027
Let's start with the most basic question.

34
00:02:13,027 --> 00:02:15,577
How many primes are there up to some number x?

35
00:02:15,577 --> 00:02:16,939
Call that count pi of x.

36
00:02:16,939 --> 00:02:19,552
The pi here has nothing to do with circles;

37
00:02:19,552 --> 00:02:22,064
it's just the letter p, for prime.

38
00:02:22,064 --> 00:02:27,114
There are four primes up to ten, and ten primes up to thirty.

39
00:02:27,239 --> 00:02:30,652
Here are the real counts, going up by powers of ten.

40
00:02:30,652 --> 00:02:34,039
Up to a thousand, there are 168 primes.

41
00:02:34,039 --> 00:02:37,652
Up to a million, 78,498.

42
00:02:37,652 --> 00:02:40,664
Up to a billion, about fifty-one million.

43
00:02:40,664 --> 00:02:43,289
Now look at the ratio, x over pi of x.

44
00:02:43,289 --> 00:02:46,527
That's the average gap between consecutive primes.

45
00:02:46,527 --> 00:02:52,089
Each time x grows by a factor of ten, the average gap grows by about 2.3,

46
00:02:52,089 --> 00:02:56,014
which is the natural logarithm of ten.

47
00:02:56,147 --> 00:03:00,047
As a teenager, Gauss noticed this pattern, and guessed that near a number

48
00:03:00,047 --> 00:03:04,297
x, the primes have density about one over the logarithm of x.

49
00:03:04,297 --> 00:03:08,960
For example, a random number near a million is prime with probability about

50
00:03:08,960 --> 00:03:10,931
one in fourteen.

51
00:03:10,931 --> 00:03:15,681
Add up that density, and you get the logarithmic integral, li of x:

52
00:03:15,681 --> 00:03:18,156
the area under the curve one over log t.

53
00:03:18,156 --> 00:03:21,314
That's the smooth curve we saw a moment ago.

54
00:03:21,314 --> 00:03:26,764
The prime number theorem, proved in 1896 by Hadamard and by de la Vallée Poussin,

55
00:03:26,764 --> 00:03:29,014
says Gauss was right in the limit:

56
00:03:29,014 --> 00:03:34,239
the ratio of pi of x to li of x tends to one.

57
00:03:34,322 --> 00:03:36,960
But how good is this approximation, really?

58
00:03:36,960 --> 00:03:42,760
Here is the error, li of x minus pi of x, all the way out to ten to the twenty-four.

59
00:03:42,760 --> 00:03:45,260
The error is tiny compared with the count.

60
00:03:45,260 --> 00:03:48,864
In fact, it has only about half as many digits.

61
00:03:48,864 --> 00:03:53,102
Half as many digits means the error is around the square root of x, or even

62
00:03:53,102 --> 00:03:54,302
a bit less.

63
00:03:54,302 --> 00:03:56,077
Does that stay true forever?

64
00:03:56,077 --> 00:03:59,189
That question is exactly the Riemann hypothesis.

65
00:03:59,189 --> 00:04:01,764
And to see why, we need Riemann's function.

66
00:04:03,953 --> 00:04:08,741
Riemann's tool was a function that Euler had studied more than a century earlier.

67
00:04:08,741 --> 00:04:13,828
Take every whole number, raise it to some power s, and add up the reciprocals.

68
00:04:13,828 --> 00:04:17,203
This is the zeta function.

69
00:04:17,311 --> 00:04:21,799
At s equals two, laying the terms end to end, they add up to pi squared over

70
00:04:21,799 --> 00:04:24,745
six, as Euler famously showed.

71
00:04:24,745 --> 00:04:27,770
At s equals one, it's the harmonic series.

72
00:04:27,770 --> 00:04:32,570
Its terms shrink too slowly, and the sum grows without bound, like the logarithm

73
00:04:32,570 --> 00:04:35,270
of the number of terms.

74
00:04:37,928 --> 00:04:41,666
Euler's great discovery was that the same function can be written using only

75
00:04:41,666 --> 00:04:42,766
the primes.

76
00:04:42,766 --> 00:04:43,978
Here's how.

77
00:04:43,978 --> 00:04:46,853
Multiply the whole sum by one over two to the s.

78
00:04:46,853 --> 00:04:51,703
Since one over n to the s, times one over two to the s, is one over two n

79
00:04:51,703 --> 00:04:55,128
to the s, that gives exactly the even terms.

80
00:04:55,128 --> 00:04:58,745
Subtract, and every even term vanishes.

81
00:04:58,745 --> 00:05:03,032
Now multiply what's left by one over three to the s, and subtract:

82
00:05:03,032 --> 00:05:05,982
every remaining multiple of three disappears.

83
00:05:05,982 --> 00:05:08,057
Then five, then seven.

84
00:05:08,057 --> 00:05:13,436
Each prime sieves out its multiples, exactly like the sieve of Eratosthenes.

85
00:05:13,436 --> 00:05:18,024
Eleven, thirteen, and every other prime each take their turn, until only the

86
00:05:18,024 --> 00:05:20,436
one at the very beginning is left.

87
00:05:20,436 --> 00:05:26,286
So zeta of s, times all of these factors, equals exactly one.

88
00:05:26,461 --> 00:05:30,049
Flip it around, and zeta becomes a product over the primes:

89
00:05:30,049 --> 00:05:32,311
Euler's product formula.

90
00:05:32,311 --> 00:05:36,136
It's really the fact that every number factors into primes in exactly one

91
00:05:36,136 --> 00:05:37,024
way.

92
00:05:37,024 --> 00:05:41,624
Each factor on the right is a geometric series, and multiplying them out produces

93
00:05:41,624 --> 00:05:47,124
every term one over n to the s exactly once, built from n's prime factors.

94
00:05:47,124 --> 00:05:51,311
The left side is about all whole numbers, the kind of thing you can do analysis

95
00:05:51,311 --> 00:05:52,024
with.

96
00:05:52,024 --> 00:05:55,911
The right side is about the primes alone.

97
00:05:56,595 --> 00:05:58,670
Euler put it to work right away.

98
00:05:58,670 --> 00:06:03,695
At s equals one, the sum on the left is the harmonic series, which diverges.

99
00:06:03,695 --> 00:06:06,670
So the product on the right must be infinite too.

100
00:06:06,670 --> 00:06:09,857
But a product of finitely many factors is finite.

101
00:06:09,857 --> 00:06:12,307
So there must be infinitely many primes:

102
00:06:12,307 --> 00:06:16,145
a new proof of Euclid's theorem, and the first hint that the primes are hidden

103
00:06:16,145 --> 00:06:17,295
inside zeta.

104
00:06:19,484 --> 00:06:22,434
Riemann's leap was to let s be a complex number:

105
00:06:22,434 --> 00:06:24,421
s equals sigma plus i t.

106
00:06:24,421 --> 00:06:27,159
But then what does n to the minus s even mean?

107
00:06:27,159 --> 00:06:28,546
Split it in two.

108
00:06:28,546 --> 00:06:32,634
n to the minus sigma is an ordinary positive number, a length.

109
00:06:32,634 --> 00:06:36,671
And n to the minus i t is e to the minus i t log n:

110
00:06:36,671 --> 00:06:41,134
a point on the unit circle, which means a rotation, by an angle of minus t

111
00:06:41,134 --> 00:06:43,984
times log n.

112
00:06:44,125 --> 00:06:46,538
So each term of zeta becomes an arrow.

113
00:06:46,538 --> 00:06:50,488
Here's sigma equal to one and a half, with t equal to zero.

114
00:06:50,488 --> 00:06:54,463
Every arrow points to the right, and placed tip to tail, they add up along

115
00:06:54,463 --> 00:06:56,150
the real line.

116
00:06:56,150 --> 00:06:57,038
Now turn up t.

117
00:06:57,038 --> 00:07:01,875
Each arrow rotates, and the n-th one turns at a rate proportional to log n,

118
00:07:01,875 --> 00:07:04,413
so the later arrows spin faster.

119
00:07:04,413 --> 00:07:10,375
The chain curls into a spiral, and its endpoint, right here, is zeta of s.

120
00:07:10,375 --> 00:07:12,675
Now slide sigma down toward one.

121
00:07:12,675 --> 00:07:17,513
The arrows shrink more slowly, and the spiral takes longer and longer to settle.

122
00:07:17,513 --> 00:07:20,113
Below one, it never settles at all.

123
00:07:20,113 --> 00:07:24,463
Keep adding terms, and the spiral just keeps unwinding outward, forever.

124
00:07:24,463 --> 00:07:26,567
The series diverges.

125
00:07:26,567 --> 00:07:29,642
And yet, look closely at that diverging spiral.

126
00:07:29,642 --> 00:07:33,717
It winds around a fixed point, and that center stays put no matter how many

127
00:07:33,717 --> 00:07:35,079
terms you add.

128
00:07:35,079 --> 00:07:38,854
You can pin it down by subtracting off the steady outward drift, which is

129
00:07:38,854 --> 00:07:41,817
N to the one minus s, over one minus s.

130
00:07:41,817 --> 00:07:46,017
When sigma is above one, this is just the ordinary sum.

131
00:07:46,017 --> 00:07:50,479
Below one, it still gives an answer, and it's the only way to extend zeta

132
00:07:50,479 --> 00:07:52,404
smoothly to these values of s.

133
00:07:52,404 --> 00:07:57,242
This is analytic continuation, and it defines zeta everywhere in the complex

134
00:07:57,242 --> 00:08:00,584
plane, except at s equals one.

135
00:08:00,584 --> 00:08:02,771
Now let's go hunting for a zero.

136
00:08:02,771 --> 00:08:05,121
Keep sigma at one half and raise t.

137
00:08:05,121 --> 00:08:06,534
Watch the center.

138
00:08:06,534 --> 00:08:12,634
At t equal to 14.1347, the spiral winds around a center that sits exactly

139
00:08:12,634 --> 00:08:13,734
at zero.

140
00:08:13,734 --> 00:08:17,259
This is the first nontrivial zero of the zeta function.

141
00:08:19,899 --> 00:08:23,736
To see all of zeta at once, we'll color the complex plane.

142
00:08:23,736 --> 00:08:27,986
Each point s gets a color for the direction that zeta of s points in, and

143
00:08:27,986 --> 00:08:29,811
a brightness for its size.

144
00:08:29,811 --> 00:08:34,507
Wherever zeta is zero, every color meets at a single dark point.

145
00:08:34,507 --> 00:08:39,094
Where the series converges, on the right, zeta is calm, close to one.

146
00:08:39,094 --> 00:08:42,974
Then analytic continuation fills in everything else.

147
00:08:42,974 --> 00:08:47,161
The bright spot at s equals one is the pole, where zeta blows up:

148
00:08:47,161 --> 00:08:49,699
that's our harmonic series again.

149
00:08:49,699 --> 00:08:54,386
On the negative real axis, there are zeros at minus two, minus four, minus

150
00:08:54,386 --> 00:08:56,049
six, and so on.

151
00:08:56,049 --> 00:08:58,311
These are called the trivial zeros.

152
00:08:58,311 --> 00:09:02,174
They're forced by a simple factor in zeta's symmetry, and they're completely

153
00:09:02,174 --> 00:09:03,724
understood.

154
00:09:03,724 --> 00:09:07,774
All the other zeros, the nontrivial ones, live inside the critical strip,

155
00:09:07,774 --> 00:09:10,574
where the real part is between zero and one.

156
00:09:10,574 --> 00:09:11,986
And look where they are:

157
00:09:11,986 --> 00:09:20,911
at heights 14.13, 21.02, 25.01, 30.42, and 32.94.

158
00:09:20,911 --> 00:09:26,299
Every one of them sits on the line where the real part is exactly one half.

159
00:09:27,907 --> 00:09:30,232
There's a deeper reason for this line.

160
00:09:30,232 --> 00:09:32,819
Riemann proved a functional equation:

161
00:09:32,819 --> 00:09:38,082
after multiplying zeta by a simple gamma-function factor, the result is unchanged

162
00:09:38,082 --> 00:09:40,232
when you swap s with one minus s.

163
00:09:40,232 --> 00:09:44,940
That's a reflection across the line where the real part is one half.

164
00:09:44,940 --> 00:09:49,665
Zeta also takes mirror-image values above and below the real axis.

165
00:09:49,665 --> 00:09:53,815
So if there were ever a zero off the line, it would have to bring three partners

166
00:09:53,815 --> 00:09:54,703
with it:

167
00:09:54,703 --> 00:09:59,990
one reflected across the critical line, and two more below.

168
00:10:00,632 --> 00:10:03,594
The Riemann hypothesis says that never happens:

169
00:10:03,594 --> 00:10:07,619
every nontrivial zero has real part exactly one half.

170
00:10:07,619 --> 00:10:12,869
In his 1859 paper, Riemann wrote that this was 'very probable', and that he

171
00:10:12,869 --> 00:10:17,982
had set aside the search for a proof, after a few fleeting attempts.

172
00:10:18,607 --> 00:10:21,132
The evidence for it is overwhelming.

173
00:10:21,132 --> 00:10:25,744
Along the critical line, zeta can be rotated into a real-valued function,

174
00:10:25,744 --> 00:10:27,519
Hardy's Z function.

175
00:10:27,519 --> 00:10:31,782
Every zero on the line is a place where this curve crosses the axis.

176
00:10:31,782 --> 00:10:33,990
Here are the first ten.

177
00:10:33,990 --> 00:10:38,965
In 1914, Hardy proved that infinitely many zeros lie on the line.

178
00:10:38,965 --> 00:10:43,115
Later work showed that a positive fraction do, more than forty percent by

179
00:10:43,115 --> 00:10:44,690
1989.

180
00:10:44,690 --> 00:10:47,878
And computers have checked the zeros one by one:

181
00:10:47,878 --> 00:10:52,903
in 2021, Platt and Trudgian verified that the first twelve trillion zeros,

182
00:10:52,903 --> 00:10:57,465
every one up to a height of three trillion, lie exactly on the line.

183
00:10:57,465 --> 00:11:02,078
This year, a preprint whose argument is credited to an AI model, Anthropic's

184
00:11:02,078 --> 00:11:06,540
Claude, checked by Levent Alpöge and Ralph Furman, pushed the proven fraction

185
00:11:06,540 --> 00:11:12,115
past two thirds, and a second, independent proof followed.

186
00:11:12,299 --> 00:11:18,274
Hilbert put the problem on his famous list in 1900, and in 2000 the Clay Mathematics

187
00:11:18,274 --> 00:11:21,524
Institute offered a million dollars for a proof.

188
00:11:21,524 --> 00:11:24,099
But why should the primes care where these zeros are?

189
00:11:30,329 --> 00:11:33,916
To see the connection, it's cleanest to count primes with weights.

190
00:11:33,916 --> 00:11:38,179
Instead of adding one at each prime, add the logarithm of p, and also count

191
00:11:38,179 --> 00:11:41,291
prime powers, like four, eight, and nine.

192
00:11:41,291 --> 00:11:45,804
This gives Chebyshev's function, psi of x, another staircase.

193
00:11:45,804 --> 00:11:47,379
The weights make it simpler:

194
00:11:47,379 --> 00:11:52,604
the prime number theorem now just says that psi of x is close to x.

195
00:11:52,779 --> 00:11:56,929
Riemann's insight, made rigorous by von Mangoldt, is an exact formula for

196
00:11:56,929 --> 00:11:58,279
this staircase.

197
00:11:58,279 --> 00:12:03,529
It's x, minus a sum over all the nontrivial zeros, rho, of x to the rho over

198
00:12:03,529 --> 00:12:06,691
rho, minus a couple of small correction terms.

199
00:12:06,691 --> 00:12:07,616
That's it.

200
00:12:07,616 --> 00:12:10,854
Exact, for every x.

201
00:12:12,487 --> 00:12:16,787
Each zero, together with its mirror image below the axis, contributes one

202
00:12:16,787 --> 00:12:17,762
wave.

203
00:12:17,762 --> 00:12:20,249
Write rho as beta plus i gamma.

204
00:12:20,249 --> 00:12:25,412
Then x to the rho is x to the beta, times e to the i gamma log x, and the

205
00:12:25,412 --> 00:12:28,724
pair of conjugate zeros adds up to a cosine.

206
00:12:28,724 --> 00:12:32,762
The height gamma sets the frequency of the wave, and the real part beta sets

207
00:12:32,762 --> 00:12:36,387
how fast its amplitude grows.

208
00:12:36,529 --> 00:12:40,479
Here's the wave from the first zero, at height 14.13.

209
00:12:40,479 --> 00:12:42,129
And here are the next two.

210
00:12:42,129 --> 00:12:46,154
On their own, they don't look like anything at all.

211
00:12:47,270 --> 00:12:52,095
But start from the smooth line, x, and subtract the waves, one zero at a time.

212
00:12:52,095 --> 00:12:52,645
One.

213
00:12:52,645 --> 00:12:53,195
Two.

214
00:12:53,195 --> 00:12:53,770
Five.

215
00:12:53,770 --> 00:12:54,433
Twenty.

216
00:12:54,433 --> 00:12:55,408
A hundred.

217
00:12:55,408 --> 00:12:56,558
A thousand.

218
00:12:56,558 --> 00:13:00,983
The line bends, and bends, until it has become the staircase, jumping by log

219
00:13:00,983 --> 00:13:03,008
p at every prime power.

220
00:13:03,008 --> 00:13:06,695
Every prime is hiding in the zeros.

221
00:13:07,304 --> 00:13:08,966
And it works in reverse.

222
00:13:08,966 --> 00:13:13,504
Take only the primes and their powers below a million, and add up cosine waves,

223
00:13:13,504 --> 00:13:15,579
one for each, with frequency log p.

224
00:13:15,579 --> 00:13:20,329
As a function of t, the total is mostly noise, except for sharp peaks.

225
00:13:20,329 --> 00:13:23,291
And the peaks fall exactly at the zeros of zeta:

226
00:13:23,291 --> 00:13:29,862
14.13, 21.02, 25.01, and on and on.

227
00:13:29,862 --> 00:13:34,262
The zeros are the frequencies of the primes, and the explicit formula is their

228
00:13:34,262 --> 00:13:35,824
Fourier series.

229
00:13:35,824 --> 00:13:38,862
The heights of the zeros decide where the steps fall.

230
00:13:38,862 --> 00:13:41,812
Their real parts decide how big the waves are.

231
00:13:41,812 --> 00:13:45,887
And that second fact is what the Riemann hypothesis is really about.

232
00:13:48,351 --> 00:13:51,101
Every wave grows like x to the beta.

233
00:13:51,101 --> 00:13:55,338
If a zero sits on the critical line, its wave grows like the square root of

234
00:13:55,338 --> 00:13:56,013
x.

235
00:13:56,013 --> 00:14:01,038
But if there were a zero with real part 0.9, its wave would grow like x to

236
00:14:01,038 --> 00:14:07,751
the 0.9, and in the long run it would dwarf every wave from the critical line.

237
00:14:07,892 --> 00:14:12,630
So the size of the error in counting primes is set by the rightmost zero.

238
00:14:12,630 --> 00:14:16,530
Call Theta the largest real part of any nontrivial zero.

239
00:14:16,530 --> 00:14:21,480
Then the error in psi of x, and in pi of x, is about x to the Theta, and no

240
00:14:21,480 --> 00:14:24,126
smaller power of x will do.

241
00:14:24,126 --> 00:14:27,151
The Riemann hypothesis says Theta is one half:

242
00:14:27,151 --> 00:14:31,938
the error is about the square root of x, the smallest it could possibly be.

243
00:14:31,938 --> 00:14:36,651
Von Koch showed in 1901 that these are exactly equivalent.

244
00:14:36,651 --> 00:14:42,026
That's the precise sense in which the primes are as regular as they can be.

245
00:14:42,659 --> 00:14:44,334
So what do we actually know?

246
00:14:44,334 --> 00:14:47,784
Theta is at least a half, since there are zeros on the line.

247
00:14:47,784 --> 00:14:51,884
And Theta is at most one, because to the right of that line the Euler product

248
00:14:51,884 --> 00:14:56,946
converges, and a convergent product of nonzero factors can't be zero.

249
00:14:56,946 --> 00:15:01,759
The prime number theorem itself, in 1896, came down to showing that there

250
00:15:01,759 --> 00:15:05,809
are no zeros on the line real-part-one.

251
00:15:06,484 --> 00:15:08,596
Then came zero-free regions.

252
00:15:08,596 --> 00:15:13,734
In 1899, de la Vallée Poussin showed there are no zeros in a thin region hugging

253
00:15:13,734 --> 00:15:14,809
that line.

254
00:15:14,809 --> 00:15:19,409
But here it is drawn to scale, with the height on a logarithmic axis.

255
00:15:19,409 --> 00:15:23,896
As you go up, the region gets thinner and thinner, like one over the logarithm

256
00:15:23,896 --> 00:15:25,292
of the height.

257
00:15:25,292 --> 00:15:30,930
In 1958, Vinogradov and Korobov found a region that shrinks more slowly, though

258
00:15:30,930 --> 00:15:35,492
with the explicit constants known today it only wins at astronomically large

259
00:15:35,492 --> 00:15:36,417
heights.

260
00:15:36,417 --> 00:15:38,780
And it still pinches toward the line.

261
00:15:38,780 --> 00:15:44,442
For more than a century, every zero-free region known has had this shape.

262
00:15:45,067 --> 00:15:48,717
And a region that pinches toward the line isn't enough to push Theta below

263
00:15:48,717 --> 00:15:49,667
one.

264
00:15:49,667 --> 00:15:53,967
Far enough up, a zero could still be hiding arbitrarily close to the line

265
00:15:53,967 --> 00:15:58,926
real-part-one, and the error term would be almost as big as x itself.

266
00:15:58,926 --> 00:16:02,676
The statement that Theta is strictly less than one, that there's a zero-free

267
00:16:02,676 --> 00:16:06,851
strip of fixed width, the same at every height, is called the quasi-Riemann

268
00:16:06,851 --> 00:16:08,176
hypothesis.

269
00:16:08,176 --> 00:16:12,426
For the primes, it says the error is at most x to some fixed power less than

270
00:16:12,426 --> 00:16:13,138
one:

271
00:16:13,138 --> 00:16:14,638
a power saving.

272
00:16:14,638 --> 00:16:19,601
Before October 2026, no one could prove that there are no zeros past real

273
00:16:19,601 --> 00:16:22,476
part 0.999999.

274
00:16:24,923 --> 00:16:28,685
There's another way to state the Riemann hypothesis, and it's the one the

275
00:16:28,685 --> 00:16:30,223
new proof uses.

276
00:16:30,223 --> 00:16:32,498
Meet the Möbius function, mu of n.

277
00:16:32,498 --> 00:16:35,960
It's zero if n is divisible by a perfect square.

278
00:16:35,960 --> 00:16:40,573
Otherwise it's plus one if n has an even number of prime factors, and minus

279
00:16:40,573 --> 00:16:43,081
one if it has an odd number.

280
00:16:43,081 --> 00:16:46,394
So six, which is two times three, gets plus one.

281
00:16:46,394 --> 00:16:48,144
Seven gets minus one.

282
00:16:48,144 --> 00:16:50,981
Twelve has a square factor, so it gets zero.

283
00:16:50,981 --> 00:16:55,756
And thirty, a product of three primes, gets minus one.

284
00:16:55,890 --> 00:16:57,415
Why this odd function?

285
00:16:57,415 --> 00:16:59,852
Flip Euler's product upside down.

286
00:16:59,852 --> 00:17:03,702
One over zeta is a product of factors one minus p to the minus s.

287
00:17:03,702 --> 00:17:05,102
Multiply it out:

288
00:17:05,102 --> 00:17:09,202
each squarefree number appears once, with a sign for each prime in it.

289
00:17:09,202 --> 00:17:11,440
Those signs are exactly mu.

290
00:17:11,440 --> 00:17:17,265
One over zeta of s is the sum of mu of n over n to the s.

291
00:17:17,973 --> 00:17:19,635
Now add up the signs.

292
00:17:19,635 --> 00:17:24,423
M of x is the running total of mu, here all the way out to ten million.

293
00:17:24,423 --> 00:17:28,685
It wanders up and down like a random walk, just like a running tally of coin

294
00:17:28,685 --> 00:17:30,015
flips.

295
00:17:30,015 --> 00:17:34,265
A random walk of x steps typically drifts about the square root of x away

296
00:17:34,265 --> 00:17:35,465
from zero.

297
00:17:35,465 --> 00:17:36,327
And so does M.

298
00:17:36,327 --> 00:17:41,665
Up to ten million, it never gets more than about 0.57 times the square root

299
00:17:41,665 --> 00:17:46,690
of x away, once x is past a couple hundred.

300
00:17:46,856 --> 00:17:48,706
Here's the remarkable fact.

301
00:17:48,706 --> 00:17:53,281
The Riemann hypothesis is true exactly when M of x stays below x to the one

302
00:17:53,281 --> 00:17:56,269
half plus epsilon, for every epsilon.

303
00:17:56,269 --> 00:18:00,881
In other words, the signs of mu are as random as coin flips, in the strongest

304
00:18:00,881 --> 00:18:01,919
sense.

305
00:18:01,919 --> 00:18:05,081
Square-root cancellation.

306
00:18:05,723 --> 00:18:08,360
Why does cancellation in mu keep zeros away?

307
00:18:08,360 --> 00:18:09,685
Follow the chain.

308
00:18:09,685 --> 00:18:14,335
Suppose the partial sums of mu grow no faster than x to some power theta.

309
00:18:14,335 --> 00:18:18,310
Then the series for one over zeta converges whenever the real part of s is

310
00:18:18,310 --> 00:18:19,835
bigger than theta.

311
00:18:19,835 --> 00:18:24,523
A convergent series gives a finite number, so one over zeta is finite there.

312
00:18:24,523 --> 00:18:27,785
But at a zero of zeta, one over zeta would be infinite.

313
00:18:27,785 --> 00:18:30,856
So there are no zeros to the right of theta.

314
00:18:30,856 --> 00:18:34,881
So to keep zeros out of a half-plane, it's enough to show the Möbius function

315
00:18:34,881 --> 00:18:35,931
cancels:

316
00:18:35,931 --> 00:18:40,294
that its sums beat the trivial bound, x, by some fixed power.

317
00:18:40,294 --> 00:18:45,381
A bound like x to the seven eighths would mean no zeros beyond seven eighths.

318
00:18:45,381 --> 00:18:49,869
That is precisely the shape of OpenAI's argument, applied to a smoothed and

319
00:18:49,869 --> 00:18:51,631
twisted version of this sum.

320
00:18:54,137 --> 00:18:57,462
Riemann's zeta is just the first of an infinite family.

321
00:18:57,462 --> 00:19:01,087
Sort the odd primes by their remainder when divided by four.

322
00:19:01,087 --> 00:19:04,825
Some are one more than a multiple of four, others one less.

323
00:19:04,825 --> 00:19:08,225
In the long run, each class gets half of the primes.

324
00:19:08,225 --> 00:19:16,504
Up to ten million, it's 332,180 against 332,398.

325
00:19:16,504 --> 00:19:20,429
But it's a race, and the three team leads almost all the time.

326
00:19:20,429 --> 00:19:25,204
It falls behind for the first time at 26,861.

327
00:19:25,204 --> 00:19:30,666
Chebyshev noticed this bias in 1853, and to understand races like this one,

328
00:19:30,666 --> 00:19:33,729
you need more than zeta.

329
00:19:33,854 --> 00:19:38,179
To separate the two classes, Dirichlet attached a repeating pattern of signs

330
00:19:38,179 --> 00:19:39,541
to the remainders:

331
00:19:39,541 --> 00:19:42,754
plus one, zero, minus one, zero.

332
00:19:42,754 --> 00:19:45,279
This is a Dirichlet character, chi.

333
00:19:45,279 --> 00:19:49,116
Put chi into the zeta series and you get an L-function, which also has an

334
00:19:49,116 --> 00:19:51,754
Euler product over the primes.

335
00:19:51,754 --> 00:19:56,341
There's one for every modulus q, and every pattern of this kind, and their

336
00:19:56,341 --> 00:20:00,554
zeros control how the primes split among the remainders mod q.

337
00:20:00,554 --> 00:20:05,241
The generalized Riemann hypothesis says that all of their nontrivial zeros

338
00:20:05,241 --> 00:20:08,929
lie on the critical line, too.

339
00:20:09,587 --> 00:20:12,287
For these L-functions there's a famous ghost.

340
00:20:12,287 --> 00:20:16,800
The classical zero-free regions leave room for exactly one possible exception:

341
00:20:16,800 --> 00:20:21,437
a single real zero, extremely close to one, for an L-function whose character

342
00:20:21,437 --> 00:20:24,387
takes only the values plus and minus one.

343
00:20:24,387 --> 00:20:26,687
It's called a Landau–Siegel zero.

344
00:20:26,687 --> 00:20:31,025
If one existed, its wave in the explicit formula would be almost as big as

345
00:20:31,025 --> 00:20:35,887
x itself, and the primes would avoid half the remainders mod q for an enormously

346
00:20:35,887 --> 00:20:37,504
long time.

347
00:20:37,504 --> 00:20:40,304
Nobody has been able to rule these ghosts out.

348
00:20:40,304 --> 00:20:45,541
Siegel proved in 1935 that they can't be too close to one, but his proof is

349
00:20:45,541 --> 00:20:46,716
ineffective:

350
00:20:46,716 --> 00:20:51,104
it can't tell you how large q has to be before the bound kicks in.

351
00:20:51,104 --> 00:20:55,387
Many theorems in number theory carry an asterisk because of this.

352
00:20:55,387 --> 00:20:59,512
That's why the new claim is stated for every Dirichlet L-function at once,

353
00:20:59,512 --> 00:21:04,012
with a single line, seven eighths, that works for every modulus.

354
00:21:04,012 --> 00:21:05,625
That kills the ghost:

355
00:21:05,625 --> 00:21:09,262
a real zero near one would sit to the right of seven eighths.

356
00:21:09,262 --> 00:21:16,112
A separate nine-page manuscript in the release also excludes these zeros directly.

357
00:21:16,729 --> 00:21:21,279
If it's correct, several old questions fall as consequences, as the manuscripts

358
00:21:21,279 --> 00:21:22,379
point out.

359
00:21:22,379 --> 00:21:25,654
The smallest number that isn't a perfect square mod a prime p would be at

360
00:21:25,654 --> 00:21:30,104
most a fixed power of log p, proving a conjecture of Vinogradov.

361
00:21:30,104 --> 00:21:34,866
Square roots mod a prime could be computed by a fast, deterministic algorithm,

362
00:21:34,866 --> 00:21:38,491
and Miller's primality test would become deterministic.

363
00:21:38,491 --> 00:21:43,779
Euler's list of 65 so-called idoneal numbers would be confirmed complete.

364
00:21:43,779 --> 00:21:48,279
And primes would be counted in every arithmetic progression with a power-saving

365
00:21:48,279 --> 00:21:49,254
error.

366
00:21:49,254 --> 00:21:54,066
Until now, all of these were known only by assuming some form of the generalized

367
00:21:54,066 --> 00:21:55,454
Riemann hypothesis.

368
00:22:01,734 --> 00:22:03,309
Now, to the news.

369
00:22:03,309 --> 00:22:08,784
On the evening of October sixth, 2026, OpenAI posted a public collection of

370
00:22:08,784 --> 00:22:15,659
722 mathematical manuscripts, grouped into 372 families of results, drawn

371
00:22:15,659 --> 00:22:19,922
from about four thousand research problems that the model was posed.

372
00:22:19,922 --> 00:22:23,951
All of it was produced by an unreleased internal model.

373
00:22:23,951 --> 00:22:29,101
According to OpenAI, most results came from the same fixed procedure, averaging

374
00:22:29,101 --> 00:22:33,239
about three hours of ChatGPT Pro thinking per result.

375
00:22:33,239 --> 00:22:37,751
They name two exceptions, and one of them is the subject of this video:

376
00:22:37,751 --> 00:22:42,301
the work on a zero-free region for the zeta function.

377
00:22:42,951 --> 00:22:47,326
Family number three is called 'The quasi-Riemann hypothesis', and it contains

378
00:22:47,326 --> 00:22:48,889
three manuscripts.

379
00:22:48,889 --> 00:22:53,164
A 199-page paper proving the line at seven eighths.

380
00:22:53,164 --> 00:22:58,551
A 49-page alternate proof of a weaker line, at eleven twelfths, whose write-up,

381
00:22:58,551 --> 00:23:02,301
OpenAI says, was edited by humans for readability.

382
00:23:02,301 --> 00:23:08,176
And a nine-page paper that rules out Landau–Siegel zeros directly.

383
00:23:08,818 --> 00:23:11,705
Here is the main theorem, as claimed.

384
00:23:11,705 --> 00:23:16,793
Every Dirichlet L-function, including the Riemann zeta function, has no zeros

385
00:23:16,793 --> 00:23:19,555
with real part greater than seven eighths.

386
00:23:19,555 --> 00:23:23,605
The same holds for a family of L-functions built on a number system called

387
00:23:23,605 --> 00:23:28,368
the Eisenstein integers, which we'll meet in a moment.

388
00:23:28,993 --> 00:23:30,943
Picture it on the critical strip.

389
00:23:30,943 --> 00:23:35,105
No zeros to the right of seven eighths, and by the mirror symmetry, none to

390
00:23:35,105 --> 00:23:37,018
the left of one eighth either.

391
00:23:37,018 --> 00:23:41,580
So every nontrivial zero is confined to a band around the critical line, the

392
00:23:41,580 --> 00:23:43,605
same band at every height.

393
00:23:43,605 --> 00:23:48,318
That's not the Riemann hypothesis, which demands the single line at one half.

394
00:23:48,318 --> 00:23:53,318
But no band of fixed width like this was ever known before.

395
00:23:53,951 --> 00:23:58,426
The release also comes with a formal proof in Lean, a programming language

396
00:23:58,426 --> 00:24:01,476
in which a computer checks every logical step.

397
00:24:01,476 --> 00:24:03,789
Here is the formal statement for zeta:

398
00:24:03,789 --> 00:24:08,439
if the real part of s is greater than seven eighths, then zeta of s is not

399
00:24:08,439 --> 00:24:09,401
zero.

400
00:24:09,401 --> 00:24:12,584
We'll come back to what has and hasn't been checked.

401
00:24:12,584 --> 00:24:15,034
So how could you possibly prove such a thing?

402
00:24:15,034 --> 00:24:19,659
The 49-page version comes with a readable outline, and its strategy turns

403
00:24:19,659 --> 00:24:22,022
out to be surprisingly concrete.

404
00:24:22,022 --> 00:24:23,209
Let's walk through it.

405
00:24:25,324 --> 00:24:28,899
The proof doesn't work directly with ordinary whole numbers.

406
00:24:28,899 --> 00:24:31,311
It moves to the Eisenstein integers:

407
00:24:31,311 --> 00:24:36,411
numbers of the form a plus b omega, where omega is a cube root of one, one

408
00:24:36,411 --> 00:24:38,936
third of the way around the unit circle.

409
00:24:38,936 --> 00:24:43,316
Together they form a triangular lattice in the complex plane.

410
00:24:43,316 --> 00:24:46,416
You can add and multiply them, just like integers.

411
00:24:46,416 --> 00:24:49,366
There are six units, numbers that divide one:

412
00:24:49,366 --> 00:24:51,291
the six sixth roots of one.

413
00:24:51,291 --> 00:24:57,416
And each has a size, its norm, a squared length that is always a whole number.

414
00:24:58,749 --> 00:25:03,161
And, crucially, every Eisenstein integer factors into Eisenstein primes in

415
00:25:03,161 --> 00:25:05,136
an essentially unique way.

416
00:25:05,136 --> 00:25:08,924
Here are the Eisenstein primes, with their six-fold symmetry.

417
00:25:08,924 --> 00:25:12,736
Some ordinary primes, like seven, split into two of them.

418
00:25:12,736 --> 00:25:16,599
Others, like five, stay prime.

419
00:25:17,249 --> 00:25:18,086
Why go there?

420
00:25:18,086 --> 00:25:20,049
First, nothing is lost.

421
00:25:20,049 --> 00:25:24,149
The Eisenstein world has its own zeta function, built by counting its numbers

422
00:25:24,149 --> 00:25:26,874
by norm, like these twelve of norm seven.

423
00:25:26,874 --> 00:25:28,111
And it factors:

424
00:25:28,111 --> 00:25:31,436
it's Riemann's zeta times a Dirichlet L-function.

425
00:25:31,436 --> 00:25:34,786
The same thing happens with every Dirichlet character.

426
00:25:34,786 --> 00:25:39,274
So if you can keep zeros out of a half-plane for the Eisenstein world's L-functions,

427
00:25:39,274 --> 00:25:44,849
you get it for zeta, and for every Dirichlet L-function, for free.

428
00:25:45,507 --> 00:25:49,495
Second, the Eisenstein integers contain the sixth roots of one, and that unlocks

429
00:25:49,495 --> 00:25:50,232
a tool:

430
00:25:50,232 --> 00:25:52,282
the sixth-power residue symbol.

431
00:25:52,282 --> 00:25:56,845
For an Eisenstein prime pi, and any u, the symbol 'u over pi, sub six' is

432
00:25:56,845 --> 00:25:59,220
always one of the six sixth roots of one.

433
00:25:59,220 --> 00:26:03,595
Here it is for every u in view, with pi equal to four plus omega, drawn as

434
00:26:03,595 --> 00:26:05,907
a little arrow pointing at the answer.

435
00:26:05,907 --> 00:26:10,395
It's the analog of asking whether u is a perfect square modulo p, but with

436
00:26:10,395 --> 00:26:13,416
six possible answers instead of two.

437
00:26:13,416 --> 00:26:16,328
Like the familiar symbol for squares, it repeats:

438
00:26:16,328 --> 00:26:20,491
shift u by any multiple of pi, and the answer doesn't change.

439
00:26:20,491 --> 00:26:22,078
It's also multiplicative.

440
00:26:22,078 --> 00:26:25,366
So the symbol of a perfect sixth power is always one:

441
00:26:25,366 --> 00:26:28,503
six times around the clock brings you back to the start.

442
00:26:28,503 --> 00:26:30,391
Keep this fact in mind.

443
00:26:30,391 --> 00:26:32,416
It's the key to the whole argument.

444
00:26:34,847 --> 00:26:36,134
Here's the plan.

445
00:26:36,134 --> 00:26:40,172
By the Möbius argument, it's enough to show that one sum cancels:

446
00:26:40,172 --> 00:26:44,434
the sum of mu of n, over Eisenstein integers n whose norm is about D.

447
00:26:44,434 --> 00:26:45,397
Call it A one of D.

448
00:26:45,397 --> 00:26:50,797
There are about D terms, each plus one, minus one, or zero, so trivially,

449
00:26:50,797 --> 00:26:52,747
it's at most D in size.

450
00:26:52,747 --> 00:26:57,497
We need it to be at most D to some fixed power less than one.

451
00:26:58,122 --> 00:27:00,947
Proving that a single sum cancels is hard.

452
00:27:00,947 --> 00:27:04,834
So instead of studying it alone, embed it in a whole family.

453
00:27:04,834 --> 00:27:09,734
For each Eisenstein integer u, twist the sum by the sixth-power symbol, u

454
00:27:09,734 --> 00:27:10,322
over n.

455
00:27:10,322 --> 00:27:15,334
That gives a family of sums, A u of D, one for each u up to some size H.

456
00:27:15,334 --> 00:27:19,772
Our sum is the member with u equal to one, since the symbol of one is always

457
00:27:19,772 --> 00:27:21,847
one.

458
00:27:22,455 --> 00:27:27,255
The crucial estimate in the paper says that this family is small on average.

459
00:27:27,255 --> 00:27:31,418
Add up the squares of all the members, and you get at most about D times H.

460
00:27:31,418 --> 00:27:35,943
With about H members, that means a typical member has size only about the

461
00:27:35,943 --> 00:27:37,443
square root of D:

462
00:27:37,443 --> 00:27:41,855
perfect cancellation, on average, just like coin flips.

463
00:27:41,855 --> 00:27:43,993
But averages can hide outliers.

464
00:27:43,993 --> 00:27:47,255
One member could still be large, and it might be ours.

465
00:27:47,255 --> 00:27:48,955
Now use the fact from before:

466
00:27:48,955 --> 00:27:51,355
the symbol of a sixth power is one.

467
00:27:51,355 --> 00:27:56,355
So when u is p to the sixth, for an Eisenstein prime p, the twist does nothing

468
00:27:56,355 --> 00:27:59,443
at all, except on the few n that are divisible by p.

469
00:27:59,443 --> 00:28:02,455
Our sum doesn't appear in the family just once.

470
00:28:02,455 --> 00:28:05,214
It appears over and over again.

471
00:28:05,214 --> 00:28:07,151
You can see it in the toy version:

472
00:28:07,151 --> 00:28:10,801
every highlighted bar lands at the same height as the first one.

473
00:28:10,801 --> 00:28:14,514
In effect, each is a copy of it.

474
00:28:15,197 --> 00:28:16,222
Now count.

475
00:28:16,222 --> 00:28:18,722
Sixth powers of primes up to size H:

476
00:28:18,722 --> 00:28:21,434
there are roughly H to the one sixth of them.

477
00:28:21,434 --> 00:28:25,322
Each copy contributes about A one squared to the total, and the total is at

478
00:28:25,322 --> 00:28:27,414
most D times H.

479
00:28:27,414 --> 00:28:28,989
So here's the squeeze.

480
00:28:28,989 --> 00:28:32,664
If our sum were large, its copies alone would eat up more and more of the

481
00:28:32,664 --> 00:28:36,101
budget, and past a certain size, they wouldn't fit.

482
00:28:36,101 --> 00:28:40,139
That size is D to the eleven twelfths.

483
00:28:40,605 --> 00:28:41,993
Now just divide.

484
00:28:41,993 --> 00:28:45,768
A one squared is at most D times H to the five sixths.

485
00:28:45,768 --> 00:28:50,293
Take H about the same size as D, and A one squared is at most D to the eleven

486
00:28:50,293 --> 00:28:51,305
sixths.

487
00:28:51,305 --> 00:28:54,780
So A one is at most D to the eleven twelfths.

488
00:28:54,780 --> 00:28:56,305
That's a power saving!

489
00:28:56,305 --> 00:29:00,705
And by the Möbius argument, it means there are no zeros to the right of eleven

490
00:29:00,705 --> 00:29:01,755
twelfths.

491
00:29:01,755 --> 00:29:04,605
The exponent comes straight from the sixth powers:

492
00:29:04,605 --> 00:29:07,043
one, minus half of one sixth.

493
00:29:07,043 --> 00:29:09,793
Everything now rests on the crucial estimate.

494
00:29:09,793 --> 00:29:12,430
Why should the whole family be small on average?

495
00:29:14,961 --> 00:29:19,173
Mean squares like this one are the territory of a tool called the large sieve.

496
00:29:19,173 --> 00:29:21,186
Here's the idea in a picture.

497
00:29:21,186 --> 00:29:24,973
If you have perpendicular directions, then the squared shadows of any vector

498
00:29:24,973 --> 00:29:29,011
on them can't add up to more than the vector's own length squared.

499
00:29:29,011 --> 00:29:33,236
Now think of each u as giving a direction, with one coordinate for every n.

500
00:29:33,236 --> 00:29:37,861
Different u's point in nearly perpendicular directions, so no single vector

501
00:29:37,861 --> 00:29:40,686
can line up with too many of them at once.

502
00:29:40,686 --> 00:29:45,461
But for sixth-power symbols, the best general large sieve loses too much.

503
00:29:45,461 --> 00:29:49,661
And on its own, it knows nothing special about the Möbius function, which

504
00:29:49,661 --> 00:29:53,286
is exactly what we can't control.

505
00:29:53,911 --> 00:29:57,736
The way out is a change of perspective, called Poisson summation.

506
00:29:57,736 --> 00:30:00,848
It trades a sum over u for a sum over a dual frequency.

507
00:30:00,848 --> 00:30:02,536
Here's the classic example.

508
00:30:02,536 --> 00:30:05,798
Sample a wide Gaussian bump at every integer, and add:

509
00:30:05,798 --> 00:30:06,973
many terms.

510
00:30:06,973 --> 00:30:10,823
Poisson summation says this equals a sum of narrow bumps, where essentially

511
00:30:10,823 --> 00:30:12,448
one term survives.

512
00:30:12,448 --> 00:30:15,136
Both sides give exactly the same number.

513
00:30:15,136 --> 00:30:20,011
Long sums turn into short ones, and short into long.

514
00:30:20,619 --> 00:30:24,394
When you Poisson-sum a character like our sixth-power symbol, what comes out

515
00:30:24,394 --> 00:30:25,782
are Gauss sums.

516
00:30:25,782 --> 00:30:30,344
A Gauss sum adds up the character's values, each multiplied by a rotating

517
00:30:30,344 --> 00:30:31,582
unit arrow.

518
00:30:31,582 --> 00:30:35,207
Here's one for a sixth-order character mod 37.

519
00:30:35,207 --> 00:30:39,519
The walk wanders around, but it always ends at distance exactly the square

520
00:30:39,519 --> 00:30:42,894
root of p from where it started.

521
00:30:43,544 --> 00:30:45,469
And now something special happens.

522
00:30:45,469 --> 00:30:49,394
An identity going back to Hasse, and used by Heath-Brown, says that mu of

523
00:30:49,394 --> 00:30:54,344
n, times the sextic Gauss sum that Poisson summation produced, equals a cubic

524
00:30:54,344 --> 00:30:57,632
Gauss sum, up to simple factors of size one.

525
00:30:57,632 --> 00:30:59,957
The Möbius function disappears.

526
00:30:59,957 --> 00:31:03,657
The randomness we couldn't control gets absorbed into Gauss sums.

527
00:31:03,657 --> 00:31:05,844
That's the reason for sixth powers:

528
00:31:05,844 --> 00:31:09,944
with ordinary quadratic symbols, the flip just hands you back another Möbius

529
00:31:09,944 --> 00:31:12,019
sum.

530
00:31:12,653 --> 00:31:15,015
Cubic Gauss sums have a history.

531
00:31:15,015 --> 00:31:20,565
In 1846, Kummer computed their angles for every prime up to 500, and found

532
00:31:20,565 --> 00:31:25,278
them bunched toward one end, in proportions of roughly three to two to one.

533
00:31:25,278 --> 00:31:27,240
Here is the same computation:

534
00:31:27,240 --> 00:31:30,178
twenty-four, fourteen, seven.

535
00:31:30,178 --> 00:31:34,915
Go further, out to sixty thousand, and the angles slowly spread out.

536
00:31:34,915 --> 00:31:39,503
Heath-Brown and Patterson proved in 1979 that in the limit they're perfectly

537
00:31:39,503 --> 00:31:40,478
even.

538
00:31:40,478 --> 00:31:42,840
But the slow drift is real too:

539
00:31:42,840 --> 00:31:47,378
it's a bias that Patterson predicted, proved assuming the generalized Riemann

540
00:31:47,378 --> 00:31:52,553
hypothesis by Dunn and Radziwiłł in 2024.

541
00:31:53,211 --> 00:31:55,673
The reason behind all of this is deep.

542
00:31:55,673 --> 00:32:00,086
Kubota and Patterson showed that cubic Gauss sums are the Fourier coefficients

543
00:32:00,086 --> 00:32:04,686
of a special function, the cubic theta function, which lives on three-dimensional

544
00:32:04,686 --> 00:32:07,986
hyperbolic space and has a hidden symmetry.

545
00:32:07,986 --> 00:32:12,673
Its simpler cousin is Jacobi's theta function, whose symmetry is exactly the

546
00:32:12,673 --> 00:32:16,136
Gaussian identity we just saw.

547
00:32:16,778 --> 00:32:21,103
Applying that symmetry turns the long sum of cubic Gauss sums into a short

548
00:32:21,103 --> 00:32:22,253
dual sum.

549
00:32:22,253 --> 00:32:24,840
And at each prime, the characters combine:

550
00:32:24,840 --> 00:32:28,928
one factor of chi to the minus one, from the Fourier side, two more from the

551
00:32:28,928 --> 00:32:33,315
theta side, and chi to the minus three is a sixth root of one cubed:

552
00:32:33,315 --> 00:32:35,540
just plus or minus one.

553
00:32:35,540 --> 00:32:40,378
The sixth-power family has turned into a quadratic one.

554
00:32:40,986 --> 00:32:43,973
And for quadratic families, there's a sharp tool:

555
00:32:43,973 --> 00:32:48,961
Heath-Brown's quadratic large sieve, from 1995, extended to number fields

556
00:32:48,961 --> 00:32:51,036
by Goldmakher and Louvel.

557
00:32:51,036 --> 00:32:55,411
It bounds mean squares of quadratic symbols with essentially no loss.

558
00:32:55,411 --> 00:32:59,786
That gives the bound we need on the dual side, and with it, the crucial estimate

559
00:32:59,786 --> 00:33:01,086
for the family.

560
00:33:01,086 --> 00:33:02,336
Well, almost.

561
00:33:04,749 --> 00:33:06,137
There's one catch.

562
00:33:06,137 --> 00:33:10,024
The theta function's coefficients aren't only cubic Gauss sums at squarefree

563
00:33:10,024 --> 00:33:11,099
indices.

564
00:33:11,099 --> 00:33:14,112
It also has coefficients at n times a cube.

565
00:33:14,112 --> 00:33:18,912
So the symmetry controls a 'completed' sum, with all the cube layers included.

566
00:33:18,912 --> 00:33:23,212
To isolate the part we want, the proof subtracts the cube layers back off,

567
00:33:23,212 --> 00:33:28,574
using Möbius inversion, the same trick Euler's sieve used.

568
00:33:29,233 --> 00:33:32,670
Small cubes are harmless, and are bounded directly.

569
00:33:32,670 --> 00:33:36,783
For the large ones, two more rounds of Poisson summation turn the leftover

570
00:33:36,783 --> 00:33:41,920
into a mean square of exactly the same kind, but with both of its size parameters

571
00:33:41,920 --> 00:33:43,883
shrunk by the same factor.

572
00:33:43,883 --> 00:33:47,733
So the problem reproduces itself at a smaller scale.

573
00:33:47,733 --> 00:33:52,608
Each round shrinks the scales by a fixed power, so after finitely many rounds

574
00:33:52,608 --> 00:33:55,883
the sums are so short that you can simply count.

575
00:33:55,883 --> 00:34:00,633
Unwinding the recursion proves the crucial estimate, and with it, the zero-free

576
00:34:00,633 --> 00:34:04,083
half-plane at eleven twelfths.

577
00:34:04,741 --> 00:34:07,016
That's the 49-page argument.

578
00:34:07,016 --> 00:34:11,491
The 199-page paper reaches eleven twelfths by a somewhat different route,

579
00:34:11,491 --> 00:34:14,991
using what it calls a zero detector, and then starts from that result and

580
00:34:14,991 --> 00:34:16,191
pushes further:

581
00:34:16,191 --> 00:34:20,878
with prime compensation, asymmetric scales, and two new moment estimates.

582
00:34:20,878 --> 00:34:24,653
Together, they move the line from eleven twelfths to seven eighths.

583
00:34:24,653 --> 00:34:26,316
The skeleton is the same:

584
00:34:26,316 --> 00:34:30,841
compare two exact ways of writing one sum, the theta reflection and Poisson

585
00:34:30,841 --> 00:34:34,466
summation, and win by a power.

586
00:34:35,183 --> 00:34:37,508
None of these ingredients is alien.

587
00:34:37,508 --> 00:34:41,970
Gauss sums, the cubic theta function, large sieves, and recursive arguments

588
00:34:41,970 --> 00:34:46,408
like this one go back to Kummer, Kubota, Patterson, Heath-Brown, and more

589
00:34:46,408 --> 00:34:50,770
recently Goldmakher, Louvel, Blomer, Dunn and Radziwiłł.

590
00:34:50,770 --> 00:34:55,083
By the paper's own account, what's new is the way the pieces are fitted together,

591
00:34:55,083 --> 00:34:58,258
with savings that hold uniformly for every character.

592
00:35:04,531 --> 00:35:05,919
So, is it right?

593
00:35:05,919 --> 00:35:09,256
As of October seventh, one day after the release:

594
00:35:09,256 --> 00:35:11,956
the manuscripts have not been peer reviewed.

595
00:35:11,956 --> 00:35:14,069
They were posted straight to GitHub.

596
00:35:14,069 --> 00:35:17,981
Outside mathematicians have only just begun to work through the human-readable

597
00:35:17,981 --> 00:35:19,248
proof.

598
00:35:19,248 --> 00:35:21,910
The strongest evidence is the formal proof.

599
00:35:21,910 --> 00:35:25,998
OpenAI's Lean library states the seven-eighths theorem for zeta, for every

600
00:35:25,998 --> 00:35:30,473
Dirichlet L-function, and for the Eisenstein family, plus the Landau–Siegel

601
00:35:30,473 --> 00:35:31,510
bound.

602
00:35:31,510 --> 00:35:35,610
Each is set up as a challenge for an independent checking tool, which allows

603
00:35:35,610 --> 00:35:40,235
only Lean's standard axioms, so no step can simply be assumed.

604
00:35:40,235 --> 00:35:46,373
The part of the library behind this result runs to about 137,000 lines.

605
00:35:46,373 --> 00:35:53,023
OpenAI itself cautions that results without a formalization could have issues.

606
00:35:53,648 --> 00:35:55,548
What does a formal proof buy you?

607
00:35:55,548 --> 00:36:00,010
If it compiles as claimed, the theorem is true exactly as stated, for the

608
00:36:00,010 --> 00:36:02,923
standard library's definition of zeta.

609
00:36:02,923 --> 00:36:04,823
No gap can hide in the logic.

610
00:36:04,823 --> 00:36:06,835
What it doesn't give you is understanding:

611
00:36:06,835 --> 00:36:09,660
why it works, and what else the method can do.

612
00:36:09,660 --> 00:36:13,173
That's what the human reading is for.

613
00:36:13,781 --> 00:36:15,631
The reactions came fast.

614
00:36:15,631 --> 00:36:17,869
The mathematician Levent Alpöge wrote:

615
00:36:17,869 --> 00:36:22,444
big, big, big, big props for quasi-Riemann and no Siegel zeroes.

616
00:36:22,444 --> 00:36:24,469
Others focused on the process.

617
00:36:24,469 --> 00:36:28,731
Andrew Sutherland of MIT said, of the release as a whole, 'We should ask for

618
00:36:28,731 --> 00:36:33,956
receipts.' Terence Tao criticized the breakneck pace at which AI is harvesting

619
00:36:33,956 --> 00:36:39,356
open problems, while Daniel Litt called it great for mathematics.

620
00:36:39,981 --> 00:36:42,006
If it holds up, what does it mean?

621
00:36:42,006 --> 00:36:46,731
It's the first zero-free region of fixed width, 130 years after the prime

622
00:36:46,731 --> 00:36:47,956
number theorem.

623
00:36:47,956 --> 00:36:52,669
The error in counting primes, in any arithmetic progression, drops to at most

624
00:36:52,669 --> 00:36:54,844
about x to the seven eighths.

625
00:36:54,844 --> 00:37:00,781
The Landau–Siegel ghost is gone, and with it come all those consequences.

626
00:37:01,415 --> 00:37:03,690
What it doesn't do is just as important.

627
00:37:03,690 --> 00:37:07,215
The Riemann hypothesis asks for the single line at one half.

628
00:37:07,215 --> 00:37:10,815
Between one eighth and seven eighths, there's still a wide band where, as

629
00:37:10,815 --> 00:37:13,877
far as this theorem knows, zeros could roam.

630
00:37:13,877 --> 00:37:15,127
In prime terms:

631
00:37:15,127 --> 00:37:18,715
at ten to the twenty-four, an error of x to the seven eighths is ten to the

632
00:37:18,715 --> 00:37:23,015
twenty-one, while the square root is ten to the twelve, a factor of a billion

633
00:37:23,015 --> 00:37:23,990
apart.

634
00:37:23,990 --> 00:37:29,415
As the paper itself says, the Riemann hypothesis remains open.

635
00:37:30,073 --> 00:37:32,310
Still, it's a remarkable moment.

636
00:37:32,310 --> 00:37:36,473
A machine-written argument, assembled from tools that number theorists built

637
00:37:36,473 --> 00:37:41,673
over the last century, may have moved one of the most stubborn walls in mathematics.

638
00:37:41,673 --> 00:37:43,723
The coming weeks of checking will tell.

639
00:37:46,396 --> 00:37:47,596
Let's recap.

640
00:37:47,596 --> 00:37:52,296
The primes look random in the small but are astonishingly regular in the large.

641
00:37:52,296 --> 00:37:57,046
Euler's product ties the zeta function to the primes, and analytic continuation

642
00:37:57,046 --> 00:37:59,908
extends it to the whole complex plane.

643
00:37:59,908 --> 00:38:04,621
The explicit formula writes the primes as a smooth trend minus one wave for

644
00:38:04,621 --> 00:38:06,212
every zero.

645
00:38:06,212 --> 00:38:10,050
The real part of the rightmost zero sets the size of the error.

646
00:38:10,050 --> 00:38:12,512
The Riemann hypothesis says one half;

647
00:38:12,512 --> 00:38:16,200
the quasi-Riemann hypothesis says anything less than one.

648
00:38:16,200 --> 00:38:20,546
Cancellation in Möbius sums is what keeps zeros away.

649
00:38:20,546 --> 00:38:25,846
The 2026 claim hides one Möbius sum in a family twisted by sixth-power symbols,

650
00:38:25,846 --> 00:38:30,708
tames the family with Poisson summation, Gauss sums, the cubic theta function

651
00:38:30,708 --> 00:38:35,096
and the quadratic large sieve, and uses the sixth-power copies to reach eleven

652
00:38:35,096 --> 00:38:38,471
twelfths, with refinements reaching seven eighths.

653
00:38:38,471 --> 00:38:41,496
It's unrefereed, but formalized in Lean.

654
00:38:41,496 --> 00:38:44,471
And the Riemann hypothesis itself is still waiting.
